    {
      "id": "287cf8e6afa723fbd1220bcf483979c35303d6e2",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 554459370,
          "type": null,
          "body": "In the long run, my inclination is to:\n\n1. make sure the metadata header can handle everything in all flavors of metadata from all capture file formats;\n1. have wiretap split off link-layer-dependent and file-type dependent metadata (not just 802.11 metadata, *all* metadata, e.g. ATM metadata) from packet data, for file formats where the metadata header is part of packet data rather than already split off in the file format;\n1. have wiretap convert metadata that's *not* part of the packet data into a similar blob of metadata;\n1. have wiretap fill in a \"common\" metadata header for all file types and encapsulation types (e.g., have it parse the radiotap header and generate the common 802.11 metadata header);\n1. split the \"encapsulation type\" for a packet into a metadata encapsulation type and a packet data encapsulation type.\n\nSo, for example, an 802.11 packet would have, regardless of how the metadata is represented:\n\n- a file-type-dependent (and encapsulation-dependent, for pcap and pcapng) blob of metadata (dependent on the encapsulation for pcap and pcapng, dependent on the file type for other formats);\n- a file-*independent* (and encapsulation-independent, for pcap and pcapng) blob of radio metadata;\n- an 802.11 packet, beginning with the 802.11 header.\n\nWiretap would provide them as three separate blobs, which libwireshark would presumably turn into two tvbuffs (maybe three, *if* it doesn't trust libwiretap to generate the file-independent blob correctly, even though they're both fellow Wireshark libraries), and would dissect all three into the protocol tree.\n\nThat way:\n\n- metadata isn't counted as packet data in various statistics;\n- it should be possible, at least in some cases, to convert link-layer X plus metadata A packets to link-layer X plus metadata B packets, e.g. converting an NCFX capture to a NetMon 802.11 capture, albeit with the risk of loss of information if a given piece of information can be stored in metadata A but not metadata B (e.g., signal strength in dBm vs. signal strength as an uninterpreted RSSI number);\n- when processing a packet with link-layer X and metadata A, writing it out with link-layer X and metadata A, you can skip the translation and write out the metadata byte-for-byte (so guaranteed to preserve information *including* information that we don't know how to interpret because the vendor doesn't publicly document it and nobody's reverse-engineered it and told us about it).\n\nIt would also mean that most users wouldn't have a reason to look at the dissection of the file-dependent metadata, as the file-independent metadata should have everything they need, and presented in a form oriented towards people reading packet traces rathe than oriented towards people writing 802.11 drivers. :-)  (The original reason why I turned the 802.11 radio metadata structure, which was used only for file formats where the radio metadata wasn't part of the packet data, ito a general 802.11 metadata structure, to be filled in by libwiretap *and* by dissectors for file/encapsulation-dependent metadata, was because somebody complained that, with radiotap, there wasn't an obvious way to indicate with which PHY/modulation/802.11 type/whatever it's called the packet was received (11b, 11g, 11a, 11n, ....)",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
